        "# Lecture 30.1: Conditional Probability\n",
        "\n",
        "# Foundations of Conditional Probability\n",
        "\n",
        "Given a underlying probability space $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$ and an event $B \\in \\mathcal{F}$ with non-zero probability ($\\mathbb{P}(B) > 0$), we can define a updated probability measure $\\mathbb{P}_B: \\mathcal{F} \\to [0, 1]$.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#def-conditional-prob}\n",
        "### Conditional Probability Measure\n",
        "For any event $A \\in \\mathcal{F}$, the conditional probability of $A$ given $B$ is defined as:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mathbb{P}[A \\mid B] = \\mathbb{P}_B(A) := \\frac{\\mathbb{P}(A \\cap B)}{\\mathbb{P}(B)}.$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### Intuition: Prior vs. Posterior\n",
        "* **Prior Probability:** $\\mathbb{P}(A)$ represents the baseline probability of event $A$ before observing any auxiliary information.\n",
        "* **Posterior Probability:** $\\mathbb{P}_B(A) = \\mathbb{P}[A \\mid B]$ represents the updated probability of event $A$ after observing that event $B$ has occurred.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# The Law of Total Probability\n",
        "\n",
        "When the sample space $\\Omega$ can be decomposed into mutually exclusive and exhaustive events, we can evaluate global probabilities by combining conditional measures.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#thm-law-total-prob}\n",
        "### Law of Total Probability (LTP)\n",
        "Let $\\{B_1, B_2, \\dots, B_n\\}$ be a partition of $\\Omega$ such that:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. $B_i \\cap B_j = \\emptyset$ for all $i \\neq j$,\n",
        "2. $\\bigcup_{j=1}^n B_j = \\Omega$,\n",
        "3. $\\mathbb{P}(B_j) > 0$ for all $j = 1, \\dots, n$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then for any event $A \\in \\mathcal{F}$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mathbb{P}(A) = \\sum_{j=1}^n \\mathbb{P}(A \\cap B_j) = \\sum_{j=1}^n \\mathbb{P}(B_j) \\, \\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_j).$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Applied Example: Unfair Coin Collection\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose you find a coin on the street selected from a mixed population:\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Fair Coins ($B_1$):** $90\\%$ of coins ($\\mathbb{P}(B_1) = 0.90$), with $\\mathbb{P}(\\text{Heads} \\mid B_1) = 0.50$.\n",
        "* **Biased Coins Type 1 ($B_2$):** $9\\%$ of coins ($\\mathbb{P}(B_2) = 0.09$), with $\\mathbb{P}(\\text{Heads} \\mid B_2) = 0.60$.\n",
        "* **Biased Coins Type 2 ($B_3$):** $1\\%$ of coins ($\\mathbb{P}(B_3) = 0.01$), with $\\mathbb{P}(\\text{Heads} \\mid B_3) = 0.80$.\n",
        "\n",
        "What is the overall probability $P(H)$ that a randomly selected coin turns up heads?\n",
        "\n",
        "Applying the Law of Total Probability:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\begin{aligned} \\mathbb{P}(H) &= \\mathbb{P}(B_1)\\mathbb{P}(H \\mid B_1) + \\mathbb{P}(B_2)\\mathbb{P}(H \\mid B_2) + \\mathbb{P}(B_3)\\mathbb{P}(H \\mid B_3) \\\\ &= (0.90)(0.50) + (0.09)(0.60) + (0.01)(0.80) \\\\ &= 0.450 + 0.054 + 0.008 = 0.512 \\quad (51.2\\%). \\end{aligned}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Bayes' Rule and Inversion of Conditioning\n",
        "\n",
        "Bayes' Rule provides the link between conditional probabilities $\\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B)$ and $\\mathbb{P}(B \\mid A)$.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#thm-bayes-rule}\n",
        "### Bayes' Rule\n",
        "Let $\\{B_1, B_2, \\dots, B_n\\}$ partition the sample space $\\Omega$. For any event $A$ with $\\mathbb{P}(A) > 0$, the posterior probability of a specific partition element $B_k$ given $A$ is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mathbb{P}(B_k \\mid A) = \\frac{\\mathbb{P}(A \\cap B_k)}{\\mathbb{P}(A)} = \\frac{\\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_k) \\, \\mathbb{P}(B_k)}{\\sum_{j=1}^n \\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_j) \\, \\mathbb{P}(B_j)}.$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Example 1: Updating the Coin Beliefs\n",
        "\n",
        "Given that the picked coin comes up heads on a single toss, what is the probability that it is the heavily biased coin ($B_3$)?\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mathbb{P}(B_3 \\mid H) = \\frac{\\mathbb{P}(H \\mid B_3) \\, \\mathbb{P}(B_3)}{\\mathbb{P}(H)} = \\frac{(0.80)(0.01)}{0.512} = \\frac{0.008}{0.512} \\approx 0.0156 \\quad (1.56\\%).$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Notice that even though $\\mathbb{P}(H \\mid B_3) = 80\\%$, $\\mathbb{P}(B_3 \\mid H) \\neq 80\\%$ due to the low prior probability of picking $B_3$.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Example 2: Demographic Inversion (Billionaires)\n",
        "\n",
        "According to *Forbes* data (April 2019), there are $2,208$ billionaires globally, of whom $1,964$ are men.\n",
        "\n",
        "* Let $B$ be the event of being a billionaire, and $M$ be the event of being male.\n",
        "* $\\mathbb{P}(M \\mid B) = \\frac{1964}{2208} \\approx 88.95\\%$.\n",
        "* Crucially, $\\mathbb{P}(M \\mid B) \\neq \\mathbb{P}(B \\mid M)$, as $\\mathbb{P}(B \\mid M)$ represents the probability that a randomly chosen male is a billionaire (which is near zero).\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Classical Fallacies & Applications\n",
        "\n",
        "## Epidemiological Fallacy (Base Rate Neglect)\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider a medical diagnostic test for HIV with the following specifications:\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Accuracy:** $99\\%$ sensitivity ($\\mathbb{P}(T \\mid H) = 0.99$) and $99\\%$ specificity ($\\mathbb{P}(T^c \\mid H^c) = 0.99$, implying a false-positive rate $\\mathbb{P}(T \\mid H^c) = 0.01$).\n",
        "* **Prevalence (Base Rate):** $0.33\\%$ of the population has HIV ($\\mathbb{P}(H) = 0.0033$).\n",
        "\n",
        "If an individual tests positive ($T$), what is the probability that they actually have HIV ($\\mathbb{P}(H \\mid T)$)?\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\begin{aligned} \\mathbb{P}(H \\mid T) &= \\frac{\\mathbb{P}(T \\mid H) \\mathbb{P}(H)}{\\mathbb{P}(T \\mid H)\\mathbb{P}(H) + \\mathbb{P}(T \\mid H^c)\\mathbb{P}(H^c)} \\\\ &= \\frac{(0.99)(0.0033)}{(0.99)(0.0033) + (0.01)(1 - 0.0033)} \\\\ &= \\frac{0.003267}{0.003267 + 0.009967} = \\frac{0.003267}{0.013234} \\approx 24.69\\%. \\end{aligned}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### Structural Insight\n",
        "Even though the false-positive rate is only $1\\%$, the healthy population $H^c$ is so much larger than $H$ ($99.67\\%$ vs. $0.33\\%$) that false positives outnumber true positives nearly $3$ to $1$. This demonstrates the high sensitivity of posterior probabilities to low prior base rates."
