        "# The Monty Hall Problem\n",
        "\n",
        "In a game show setting with 3 closed doors:\n",
        "\n",
        "* Behind one door is a valuable prize (a car), while the other two hide nothing of value (goats).\n",
        "* Let $B_i = \\{\\text{The car is behind Door } i\\}$ for $i \\in \\{1, 2, 3\\}$.\n",
        "* Prior probabilities before any door is opened:\n",
        "  $$\\mathbb{P}(B_1) = \\mathbb{P}(B_2) = \\mathbb{P}(B_3) = \\frac{1}{3}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Game Setup and Event Definitions\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Player Choice:** Without loss of generality, assume you originally choose **Door #1**.\n",
        "2. **Host Action:** Monty, who knows what is behind all doors, must open one of the two unchosen doors (#2 or #3) to reveal a goat.\n",
        "3. **Observed Event:** Let $A = \\{\\text{Monty opens Door \\#2}\\}$.\n",
        "4. **Decision:** Should you stick with Door #1 or switch to Door #3?\n",
        "\n",
        "We evaluate the posterior probability $\\mathbb{P}(B_3 \\mid A)$ that the car is behind Door #3 given that Monty opened Door #2.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Host Behavior Conditional Probabilities\n",
        "\n",
        "To apply Bayes' Rule, we determine $\\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_i)$ for each hypothesis $B_i$:\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Case 1 ($B_1$ is true):** If the car is behind Door #1, both Door #2 and Door #3 hide goats. Monty chooses randomly between them with equal probability:\n",
        "  $$\\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_1) = \\frac{1}{2}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Case 2 ($B_2$ is true):** If the car is behind Door #2, Monty cannot open Door #2 because he must reveal a goat:\n",
        "  $$\\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_2) = 0$$\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Case 3 ($B_3$ is true):** If the car is behind Door #3, Monty cannot open Door #1 (your choice) or Door #3 (car location). He is forced to open Door #2:\n",
        "  $$\\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_3) = 1$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Posterior Calculation via Bayes' Rule\n",
        "\n",
        "Applying Bayes' Theorem to compute $\\mathbb{P}(B_3 \\mid A)$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{P}(B_3 \\mid A) = \\frac{\\mathbb{P}(B_3 \\cap A)}{\\mathbb{P}(A)} = \\frac{\\mathbb{P}(B_3) \\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_3)}{\\mathbb{P}(B_1)\\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_1) + \\mathbb{P}(B_2)\\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_2) + \\mathbb{P}(B_3)\\mathbb{P}(A \\mid B_3)}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Substituting the known probabilities into the formula:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{P}(B_3 \\mid A) = \\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\cdot 1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(0) + \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(1)} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{\\frac{1}{6} + 0 + \\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{3}}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{2}{3}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Similarly, for staying with your original choice:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{P}(B_1 \\mid A) = \\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{6}}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{3}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $\\mathbb{P}(B_3 \\mid A) = \\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\mathbb{P}(B_1 \\mid A) = \\frac{1}{3}$, **you should switch doors**. Switching doubles your chance of winning the car."
